The cooking window tells you which dish you get, but not how good it will be. Project Cook adds the numbers. Each dish card of the "THIS POT" panel shows one line for each quality level that can occur, with the chance of that level and what the dish gives at that level. So you can see if one more seasoning or a better ingredient is worth it, before the ingredients are gone.

With the default settings, Project Cook only changes what the cooking window shows. It does not change the cooking result, the recipes, or your save.

[size=5]Main features[/size]
[list]
[*][b]Quality chances.[/b] One line for each quality level (Perfect, Good, Average, Failed) with its chance in percent. Levels that cannot occur do not show.[/*]
[*][b]What you get for each level.[/b] Satiety, morale, stamina, and life that your character gets from the whole dish at that quality, with its eat talents and the Life factor of the difficulty (not temporary buffs).[/*]
[*][b]Portion count.[/b] Shows when the dish splits into more than one portion.[/*]
[*][b]Talents included.[/b] The chances include the cooking talents of your character, for example "Practice Makes Perfect".[/*]
[*][b]Dish card tooltip.[/b] Point at a dish card to see the tier of the dish, the cooking XP for that dish, and its trade value at each quality level.[/*]
[*][b]Ingredient tooltips.[/b] Point at an ingredient in the cooking window to see its tier (High-end, Mid-tier, Low-grade), its trade value, and its raw stats.[/*]
[*][b]Food sort.[/b] A fridge and a Cooking storage get a dropdown in the storage window and in the cooking window (also the Backpack): sort by Satiety, Morale, Stamina, Life, Trade value, or Expiration Date, with the number on each item (also on the workbench). Expired food comes first, with the days until it spoils in red. Only the view changes; the items stay in their places in the storage.[/*]
[*][b]Cooking tag.[/b] Tag any storage with Cooking (Status row of "What goes in here"), and its items show as a tab in the cooking window. A storage with the game's Food tag shows as a tab too. It does not change the robot or how fast food spoils.[/*]
[*][b]Tier badge.[/b] The food badge of an ingredient shows its tier: gold with a triangle up is High-end, grey with a triangle down is Low-grade. The unchanged green badge means Mid-tier or no tier.[/*]
[*][b]Drink previews.[/b] The Juicer and Coffee Machine show quality chances, drink stats, portion counts, tooltips, and ingredient tier badges.[/*]
[*][b]English and Chinese.[/b] Added text follows the game language.[/*]
[/list]

Trade values include your appraisal talents, and not the demand of a trader.

[size=5]How the ingredient tier works[/size]
[b]Quality and tier are separate.[/b] Quality is the cooking result: Perfect, Good, Average, or Failed. Tier is High-end, Mid-tier, or Low-grade and matters for recipes that use ingredient types, such as Meat + Vegetables. A High-end dish can still turn out Failed, and a Low-grade dish can turn out Perfect. Project Cook shows the dish tier and the chance of each quality.
[list]
[*]The tier matters only for a recipe that asks for ingredient types (for example Meat + Vegetables). A recipe that asks for specific items has fixed stats and ignores the tier.[/*]
[*]The dish gets the tier of the highest-tier ingredient used by the recipe. One High-end ingredient is enough; more of them add nothing to the tier. Cooking talents can also raise the dish tier.[/*]
[*]The tier multiplies the ingredient totals for satiety, health, and life: High-end x1.7, Mid-tier x1.4, Low-grade x1.1. Quality applies another multiplier, and satiety also gets a flat bonus per ingredient. The tier also selects the dish (for example Eight-Treasure Stew, Stew, or Simple Stew), and with it the base trade value.[/*]
[*]The multiplier works on the sum of the ingredient stats. A High-end ingredient with weak stats (for example Enoki Mushrooms) can raise the tier and the trade value, and still lower the stats. The dish card shows the final numbers, so compare there.[/*]
[*]The tier of an ingredient comes from its price. Staple food, snacks, and drinks have no tier.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Limitations[/size]
[list]
[*]Cooking previews are not available in hot pot mode.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Requirements[/size]
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/survivallog/mods/12]BepInEx for Survival Log[/url]. Install it first.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Installation[/size]
[list=1]
[*]Install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/survivallog/mods/12]BepInEx[/url] [i](if no other mods were installed before, start the game once so BepInEx finishes setup, then quit)[/i].[/*]
[*]Extract this mod's zip into the game folder (the folder with the game .exe). The DLL lands in BepInEx\plugins. Full path example:
[code]C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\Survival Log\BepInEx\plugins\ProjectCook.dll[/code]
[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Uninstall[/size]
Delete [font=Courier New]ProjectCook.dll[/font] from the [font=Courier New]BepInEx\plugins[/font] folder. The game then drops the Cooking tag. A storage with only the Cooking tag loses its rule, so give it a game tag (for example Food) before you uninstall.

